(Nearly a year later, in the month of October, 18ó, London was startled by a crime of) Tj T*
(singular ferocity and rendered all the more notable by the high position of the) Tj T*
(victim. The details were few and startling. A maid servant living alone in a house) Tj T*
(not far from the river, had gone upstairs to bed about eleven. Although a fog rolled) Tj T*
(over the city in the small hours, the early part of the night was cloudless, and the) Tj T*
(lane, which the maidís window overlooked, was brilliantly lit by the full moon. It) Tj T*
(seems she was romantically given, for she sat down upon her box, which stood) Tj T*
(immediately under the window, and fell into a dream of musing. Never \(she used to) Tj T*
(say, with streaming tears, when she narrated that experience\), never had she felt) Tj T*
(more at peace with all men or thought more kindly of the world. And as she so sat) Tj T*
(she became aware of an aged beautiful gentleman with white hair, drawing near along) Tj T*
(the lane; and advancing to meet him, another and very small gentleman, to whom at) Tj T*
(first she paid less attention. When they had come within speech \(which was just) Tj T*
(under the maidís eyes\) the older man bowed and accosted the other with a very pretty) Tj T*
(manner of politeness. It did not seem as if the subject of his address were of great) Tj T*
(importance; indeed, from his pointing, it sometimes appeared as if he were only) Tj T*
(inquiring his way; but the moon shone on his face as he spoke, and the girl was) Tj T*
(pleased to watch it, it seemed to breathe such an innocent and old-world kindness of) Tj T*
(disposition, yet with something high too, as of a well-founded self-content.) Tj T*
(Presently her eye wandered to the other, and she was surprised to recognise in him a) Tj T*
(certain Mr. Hyde, who had once visited her master and for whom she had conceived a) Tj T*
(dislike. He had in his hand a heavy cane, with which he was trifling; but he) Tj T*
(answered never a word, and seemed to listen with an ill-contained impatience. And) Tj T*
(then all of a sudden he broke out in a great flame of anger, stamping with his foot,) Tj T*
(brandishing the cane, and carrying on \(as the maid described it\) like a madman. The) Tj T*
(old gentleman took a step back, with the air of one very much surprised and a trifle) Tj T*
(hurt; and at that Mr. Hyde broke out of all bounds and clubbed him to the earth. And) Tj T*
(next moment, with ape-like fury, he was trampling his victim under foot and hailing) Tj T*
(down a storm of blows, under which the bones were audibly shattered and the body) Tj T*
(jumped upon the roadway. At the horror of these sights and sounds, the maid fainted.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It was two oíclock when she came to herself and called for the police. The murderer) Tj T*
(was gone long ago; but there lay his victim in the middle of the lane, incredibly) Tj T*
(mangled. The stick with which the deed had been done, although it was of some rare) Tj T*
(and very tough and heavy wood, had broken in the middle under the stress of this) Tj T*
(insensate cruelty; and one splintered half had rolled in the neighbouring gutteróthe) Tj T*
